een able to have  with this sort of new approach to  fundamental physics  and that's fairly typical you know i  think had i even stayed sort of doing uh  physics as i did when i was a teenager  and a little bit beyond um you know i'm  quite certain that the physics project  we've now done would never have happened  um and or at least wouldn't have  happened under my  uh under my sort of uh leadership  and um  the uh it might not have happened for  another 50 or 100 years  um but it's um it's it's basically  because i've done lots of different  things which somehow one can use the  ideas from those other things on the  thing that one was on the original thing  rather than the sort of main line of  progressive innovation i mean typically  as i say when there are new  methodologies you've got five to five to  10 to 20 years when that new methodology  in some particular field is like really  hot you're figuring out lots of stuff  you know we've seen that with machine  learning for example in in the kind of  computational area you know a lot of  stuff gets worked out  in a period of five or ten years and  then it's a slower story after that  um and many are many many many other  examples that can be given of that  um and i think that's uh so  and then as i say it can be 50 or 100  years before you get the next shot in  that particular area  um  anyway i should i should probably wrap  up here in a moment but um  uh  yes i think i need to go but um parker  is commenting her on um  uh predicting the future and what the  future will be like um we'll talk about  that another time it's an interesting  thing i've i've had a you know a solid  50-year  span when i've been able to kind of make  predictions and see what happened with  them and i'd say one thing that's  interesting is there are inexorable  predictions about the future things  where i can just tell you this is what's  gonna happen it's like chug chug chug  chug chug you know that's how things are  going to evolve you can see the seeds of  something and you can see that over time  that thing will eventually become a big  thing uh even some things i've built  myself  i can see the inexorable future that you  know 50 years 100 years whatever those  seeds will have grown into big things um  you know it'd be nice if it happened  sooner but but uh you know might not  um where and then there are things where  it's a big surprise like just didn't  expect that  um and it's sort of an interesting  interplay between the things which are  the inexorable future and the things  which are the surprises of the future  and sort of recognizing where there  might be surprises is an interesting  interesting problem  i need to go and  look at some things actually that are  related a bit to building some of the  future of computation  um i think i should wrap up here and  thanks for a lot of interesting  questions and comments  and uh  i look forward to doing this again  hopefully next week
 

